  <h2>What is Polki?</h2>
  <p>Polki refers to diamonds in their uncut form, set in gold. Instead of the many facets of a modern brilliant cut, Polki stones keep a flatter, natural form, which gives a warm, candle-like shine rather than sharp sparkle. The style traces back to royal Rajasthani and Mughal courts.</p>

  <h2>How a Polki piece is made</h2>
  <p>Each Polki stone is shaped by hand, then set into a gold base, traditionally using the <a href="/jadau-jewellery">Jadau</a> technique with fine gold foil. The back of many Polki pieces is finished with meenakari (enamel) work, so the jewellery is beautiful on both sides. Because every stone is individually shaped, no two Polki pieces are exactly alike.</p>

  <h2>Polki at Vaishnavi Jewels</h2>
  <p>We make Polki chokers, raani haars and long necklaces, full bridal sets, earrings and jhumkas, maang tikkas, bangles and kadas, often combined with emeralds, rubies and pearls. Pieces range from heavy bridal sets to light festive and gifting pieces.</p>

  <h2>Customisation</h2>
  <p>Designs can be adjusted in size, stone colours and layout to suit your outfit and occasion. Visit the showroom or book a video call to see pieces up close. Planning a wedding? See <a href="/bridal-jewellery">Bridal jewellery</a>.</p>

  <p>Read more: <a href="/blog/polki-vs-jadau-guide">Polki vs Jadau &ndash; what's the difference?</a></p>
</InfoPage>
